  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 Materials Cloud dataset is an early but clearly reusable record: it is linked to six associated articles and one additional biomedical article mention, giving it seven documented downstream reuse events. The dataset sits within a broader computational and experimental workflow on CrCl\u2083/NbSe\u2082 heterostructures, and its stewardship record also connects it to related software and companion articles. So far, the public attention signals are quiet, with no citations recorded in OpenAlex and no views or downloads captured in the available DataCite record.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset \u201cOne dimensional edge localized YSR states in CrCl\u2083 on NbSe\u2082\u201d was published in 2025 as an open-access scholarly dataset from a Utrecht University team. Its content supports research on magnet/superconductor hybrid systems, specifically the edge-localized Yu-Shiba-Rusinov states observed in ferromagnetic monolayer CrCl\u2083 on superconducting NbSe\u2082. The abstract and linked resources indicate that the dataset is part of a larger research and computational package rather than a standalone deposit.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest measurable signal in the record is reuse. The dataset is documented as being reused in seven downstream instances, including six associated articles and one biomedical article mention. That pattern suggests the dataset has already been integrated into later scholarly work, even though the citation record itself is still at zero in OpenAlex. Because the citation base is still absent, the dataset\u2019s field standing should be treated as too early to interpret quantitatively.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The stewardship record also points to a well-developed research infrastructure around the dataset. It is connected by DataCite relationships to several journal articles and software resources, including SIESTA, TB2J, and the aiida_siesta plugin, which places the dataset in an established computational ecosystem. This kind of linkage matters for reuse because it makes the data easier to situate, reproduce, and extend within related work on condensed matter physics and superconductivity.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the broader attention record is sparse. DataCite currently shows zero views and zero downloads, scite reports no citation activity, and public discourse sources are unavailable or empty in the provided record. That combination does not weaken the reuse evidence already present, but it does indicate that observable uptake outside formal reuse links remains limited so far. Overall, the dataset\u2019s story is one of early scholarly integration and solid research infrastructure, with impact emerging through documented reuse rather than citation volume or public visibility.</p>",
